WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:05.220
With Star Wars: Episode 9 - The Rise of Skywalker,
The Skywalker Saga has finally come to an

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:08.360
end — but how does it stack up to other
entries in the series?

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:12.840
Let's rank every Star Wars film from worst
to best, according to the scores from critics

00:00:12.840 --> 00:00:13.840
on Rotten Tomatoes.

00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:19.780
Fans were absolutely thrilled in 1999 when
Star Wars creator George Lucas returned after

00:00:19.780 --> 00:00:24.100
a 17-year absence and gave us The Phantom
Menace — at least, they were thrilled until

00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:25.580
they actually watched the thing.

00:00:26.020 --> 00:00:27.720
There were so many problems with this one.

00:00:27.910 --> 00:00:29.040
Glacial pacing.

00:00:29.050 --> 00:00:30.050
Clunky dialogue.

00:00:30.050 --> 00:00:34.100
And probably the silliest, most distracting
character in the entire Star Wars mythos.

00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:37.220
We're speaking, of course, of a certain Jar
Jar Binks.

00:00:37.860 --> 00:00:43.720
"Something something, this way, hurry."

00:00:44.020 --> 00:00:48.800
Many reviewers felt The Phantom Menace committed
a cardinal sin: Namely, it was deathly boring.

00:00:48.960 --> 00:00:52.979
In his lacerating review, The Wall Street
Journal's Joe Morgenstern took the film to

00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:55.680
task for getting bogged down in politics,
writing:

00:00:55.980 --> 00:01:00.240
"What I can't comprehend is why the political
details had to be so tedious and abstract.

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:04.000
Will the kids of our nation and the world
truly be titillated by trade wars and the

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:05.880
spectacle of a do-nothing Senate?"

00:01:06.160 --> 00:01:10.580
Despite the inclusion of a thrilling pod race
and an epic three-way lightsaber fight, The

00:01:10.580 --> 00:01:14.020
Phantom Menace was a disappointment that many
fans could've done without.

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:17.860
The film ultimately scored a dismal 53% rating
on Rotten Tomatoes.

00:01:18.740 --> 00:01:20.740
What's worse than an unsatisfying beginning?

00:01:20.940 --> 00:01:22.260
How about a sloppy ending?

00:01:22.600 --> 00:01:26.340
Unfortunately, many critics felt like The
Rise of Skywalker was ultimately something

00:01:26.340 --> 00:01:27.160
of a slog.

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:31.500
Reviewers generally felt that the film failed
to take any of the chances that defined its

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:34.100
predecessor, 2017's The Last Jedi.

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:36.800
But that wasn't the only grievance by a long
shot.

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:40.920
In her decidedly negative review, Chicago
Tribune's Katie Walsh wrote:

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:45.630
"[Director J.J. Abrams] hits the gas on this
space race right away and never pauses to

00:01:45.630 --> 00:01:50.020
let a single emotion land, because the characters
are too busy scrambling around the galaxy

00:01:50.020 --> 00:01:51.980
looking for thingamajigs and each other.

00:01:52.340 --> 00:01:55.040
The pace and volume of plot is punishing,
and numbing."

00:01:55.380 --> 00:01:59.740
The fact that the film shamelessly undid several
plot points from The Last Jedi was seen as

00:01:59.740 --> 00:02:00.960
another major flaw.

00:02:01.180 --> 00:02:01.740
As A.A.

00:02:01.740 --> 00:02:03.380
Dowd wrote in his AV Club review:

00:02:03.800 --> 00:02:08.500
"[Abrams has] made what feels sometimes like
a glorified apology for his successor’s

00:02:08.500 --> 00:02:09.000
choices."

00:02:09.300 --> 00:02:14.440
As of the making of this video, the hotly-anticipated
Rise of Skywalker has only scored around a

00:02:14.440 --> 00:02:15.880
58% on Rotten Tomatoes.

00:02:16.240 --> 00:02:18.180
It's hard to believe… but here we are.

00:02:18.880 --> 00:02:22.940
Star Wars: Episode II - Attack of the Clones
focused on the theoretically epic love story

00:02:22.940 --> 00:02:27.800
between Anakin Skywalker — played by Hayden
Christensen -- and Padmé Amidala — played

00:02:27.800 --> 00:02:28.880
by Natalie Portman.

00:02:29.220 --> 00:02:33.180
Unfortunately, their romance brought the movie
to a screeching halt whenever they had a scene

00:02:33.180 --> 00:02:33.680
together.

00:02:34.080 --> 00:02:36.360
That lack of chemistry was only part of the
problem.

00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:39.620
George Lucas's dialogue has never been this
jarringly bad.

00:02:39.740 --> 00:02:44.460
On the upside, many reviewers felt the film
was somewhat redeemed by the crackerjack action

00:02:44.460 --> 00:02:44.960
sequences.

00:02:45.080 --> 00:02:46.960
AV Club's Scott Tobias wrote that,

00:02:47.340 --> 00:02:51.770
"Save for a crowd-pleasing moment with Yoda,
the movie remains pretty and inert, with no

00:02:51.770 --> 00:02:54.020
authentic emotion, no cumulative power."

00:02:54.480 --> 00:02:59.040
Meanwhile, The Wall Street Journal's Joe Morgenstern
specifically called out George Lucas's wooden

00:02:59.040 --> 00:03:00.080
dialogue, writing:

00:03:00.220 --> 00:03:03.960
"You can't blame actors, particularly young
ones, when the words put in their mouths are

00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:08.460
almost unspeakable and the direction seems
to suck them dry of energy or spontaneity."

00:03:08.840 --> 00:03:12.960
After suffering this attack of the critics,
Attack of the Clones wound up with a disappointing

00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:15.180
65% rating on Rotten Tomatoes.

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:20.360
Solo was famously rescued by director Ron
Howard after original filmmakers Phil Lord

00:03:20.360 --> 00:03:24.360
and Chris Miller departed — and the fact
that the film needed rescuing definitely shows

00:03:24.360 --> 00:03:25.360
in the final product.

00:03:25.360 --> 00:03:26.360
"Come on, Chewie.

00:03:26.700 --> 00:03:28.480
Gonna need a little of that Wookie oomph."

00:03:28.800 --> 00:03:29.360
"Murh."

00:03:29.420 --> 00:03:31.660
In his review for The Beat, Kyle Pinion noted,

00:03:31.800 --> 00:03:35.520
"[The filmmakers] are working overtime to
recreate the Han you know and love from the

00:03:35.520 --> 00:03:40.040
original trilogy rather than crafting a younger,
more formative version from which he can evolve.

00:03:40.380 --> 00:03:44.080
In short, it’s clearly a movie made by committee
with many hands on deck."

00:03:44.540 --> 00:03:48.440
Looking past the film's troubled production
history, most reviewers thought Solo was a

00:03:48.440 --> 00:03:51.300
solid, if not altogether spectacular installment.

00:03:51.460 --> 00:03:54.480
Critics generally felt its biggest flaw was
failing to stand out.

00:03:54.840 --> 00:03:56.780
Allison Rose of FlickDirect wrote:

00:03:57.140 --> 00:04:01.780
"As a standalone film, Solo: A Star Wars Story
has a good plot, interesting characters, and

00:04:01.780 --> 00:04:03.040
well-done action scenes.

00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:07.520
As part of the classic franchise, it comes
across a little disjointed and disconnected."

00:04:08.080 --> 00:04:13.300
Solo: A Star Wars Story holds a decidedly
so-so 70% rating on Rotten Tomatoes.

00:04:14.020 --> 00:04:18.120
Widely considered the best entry of the prequel
trilogy, Revenge of the Sith benefited from

00:04:18.120 --> 00:04:21.760
an emphasis on action — and on Anakin Skywalker's
turn to the Dark Side.

00:04:22.420 --> 00:04:26.740
It's a character development that Hayden Christensen
pulls off with aplomb, and most critics were

00:04:26.740 --> 00:04:27.840
quick to praise the film.

00:04:28.310 --> 00:04:31.460
In his glowing review, Toledo Blade's Christopher
Borrelli wrote:

00:04:31.470 --> 00:04:35.840
"[Revenge of the Sith is] a terrific, riveting,
surprisingly affecting finale to a series

00:04:35.840 --> 00:04:38.260
that has impacted every last inch of our culture."

00:04:38.260 --> 00:04:40.860
But not every critic was thrilled by Revenge
of the Sith.

00:04:41.100 --> 00:04:43.080
Christopher Tookey of the Daily Mail wrote:

00:04:43.260 --> 00:04:48.310
"Episode III has the same faults as episodes
I and II (terrible dialogue and worse acting)

00:04:48.310 --> 00:04:53.190
plus two brand new ones — a confusing lack
of geographical sense and a tendency to leave

00:04:53.190 --> 00:04:54.360
gaping plot holes.

00:04:54.560 --> 00:04:56.900
[...] I found the whole thing a colossal disappointment."

00:04:57.180 --> 00:04:59.600
But at the end of the day, most critics were
quite fond of the film.

00:04:59.880 --> 00:05:02.220
John P. McCarthy of ReelTalk Reviews wrote:

00:05:02.440 --> 00:05:06.810
"The saga comes full circle as both the Empire
and the seeds of the rebellion are born, and

00:05:06.810 --> 00:05:09.520
the original magic of 1977 is rediscovered.

00:05:09.840 --> 00:05:13.880
[George Lucas] taps into the old-fashioned,
Saturday matinee quality he brought to the

00:05:13.880 --> 00:05:16.620
first Star Wars along with revolutionary craftsmanship."

00:05:17.040 --> 00:05:21.570
After all was said and done, Revenge of the
Sith wound up with an 80% rating on Rotten

00:05:21.570 --> 00:05:22.080
Tomatoes.

00:05:22.580 --> 00:05:26.460
At the time of its release, Return of the
Jedi was the most highly-anticipated movie

00:05:26.460 --> 00:05:27.200
of all time.

00:05:27.500 --> 00:05:31.440
As such, it couldn't help but let a few fans
down — although generally speaking, it was

00:05:31.440 --> 00:05:35.040
considered a satisfying if imperfect ending
to the original trilogy.

00:05:35.420 --> 00:05:39.140
In her highly positive review, Sheila Benson
of the Los Angeles Times wrote,

00:05:39.660 --> 00:05:43.980
"With this last of the central Star Wars cycle,
there is the sense of the closing of a circle,

00:05:43.980 --> 00:05:45.680
of leaving behind real friends.

00:05:45.900 --> 00:05:50.300
It is accomplished with a weight and a new
maturity that seem entirely fitting, yet the

00:05:50.300 --> 00:05:54.300
movie has lost none of its sense of fun; it
bursts with new inventiveness.

00:05:54.560 --> 00:05:59.020
With Jedi, George Lucas may have pulled off
the first triple crown of motion pictures."

00:05:59.320 --> 00:06:01.180
But not every critic was convinced.

00:06:01.360 --> 00:06:05.320
Arthur Knight of The Hollywood Reporter acknowledged
the impressive visuals, but he straight-up

00:06:05.320 --> 00:06:06.700
panned the film, writing that:

00:06:06.970 --> 00:06:11.280
"It conveys the sense that the machinery has
already started to wear down, and the inventiveness

00:06:11.280 --> 00:06:12.040
to wear thin.

00:06:12.460 --> 00:06:14.260
To be sure, the film abounds in action.

00:06:14.520 --> 00:06:19.700
[...] But, despite its huge cast of new intergalactic
grotesques, Jedi seems woefully familiar."

00:06:20.180 --> 00:06:23.980
But most critics thought Return of the Jedi
was a solid entry — perhaps not as good

00:06:23.980 --> 00:06:26.840
as people had hoped for, but none too terrible,
either.

00:06:27.180 --> 00:06:30.280
In his review for the San Francisco Chronicle,
Peter Stack wrote:

00:06:30.560 --> 00:06:34.640
"Though it looks almost too polished, a handful
of eye-smacking action scenes were breakthroughs

00:06:34.640 --> 00:06:36.480
in precomputer cinematic graphics.

00:06:36.760 --> 00:06:40.540
And when the film moves, it does so with blazing
energy and awesome noise.

00:06:40.900 --> 00:06:44.920
[...] Seeing the very end of an endlessly
hyped trilogy somehow puts a lump in the throat."

00:06:45.100 --> 00:06:49.360
Return of the Jedi currently holds a respectable
82% rating on Rotten Tomatoes.

00:06:50.180 --> 00:06:54.500
Rogue One: A Star Wars Story follows the plucky
band of rebels who died getting the plans

00:06:54.500 --> 00:06:58.460
for the Empire's infamous space station, the
Death Star, into the hands of the Rebellion.

00:06:58.680 --> 00:07:02.850
The film popped in all the right places, was
bolstered by terrific acting and production

00:07:02.850 --> 00:07:05.560
design, and featured a truly jaw-dropping
finale.

00:07:05.800 --> 00:07:09.560
Many critics felt Rogue One was the first
entry in the franchise that actually felt

00:07:09.570 --> 00:07:14.240
like a war movie, while others bemoaned the
myriad acts of fan service and the occasionally

00:07:14.240 --> 00:07:15.280
sluggish pacing.

00:07:15.420 --> 00:07:17.240
"I'm one with The Force, The Force is with
me.

00:07:17.240 --> 00:07:18.640
I'm one with The Force…"

00:07:18.640 --> 00:07:19.140
"Pray?"

00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:22.100
Nonetheless, critics were generally way into
this movie.

00:07:22.640 --> 00:07:25.220
In her glowing review, Newshub's Kate Rodger
wrote:

00:07:25.220 --> 00:07:29.500
"[Rogue One is] dark, gritty, dirty, brave
and a Star Wars story worth telling.

00:07:29.690 --> 00:07:34.020
No, it's not perfect, [...] But it delivers
us so deliciously to the door of A New Hope

00:07:34.030 --> 00:07:37.901
and it's jam-packed with everything I need
for a thrilling few hours in that galaxy far,

00:07:37.901 --> 00:07:38.720
far away."

00:07:39.260 --> 00:07:44.200
At final tally, Rogue One: A Star Wars Story
earned an 83% rating on Rotten Tomatoes.

00:07:44.200 --> 00:07:45.300
That's none too shabby.

00:07:46.160 --> 00:07:50.000
It certainly divided fans, but most critics
really loved The Last Jedi.

00:07:50.280 --> 00:07:54.420
The middle chapter of the sequel trilogy — and
the only one to be directed by Rian Johnson

00:07:54.420 --> 00:07:58.860
rather than J.J. Abrams — definitely made
some controversial choices with the narrative.

00:07:59.020 --> 00:08:03.160
But refreshingly, the film didn't play it
safe, and critics really appreciated that.

00:08:03.540 --> 00:08:07.120
In his review for ComicBookMovie.com, Josh
Wilding wrote:

00:08:07.320 --> 00:08:11.760
"A superb addition to the Skywalker Saga which
is sure to leave fans with plenty to talk

00:08:11.770 --> 00:08:16.419
— and argue — about, Star Wars: The Last
Jedi now stands proudly alongside The Empire

00:08:16.420 --> 00:08:19.600
Strikes Back as one of the greatest sci-fi
movies of all-time."

00:08:19.760 --> 00:08:23.900
Sure, The Last Jedi got its share of negative
feedback too, although the majority of reviewers

00:08:23.900 --> 00:08:24.680
praised the film.

00:08:25.220 --> 00:08:28.180
In his review for Parade, Samuel R. Murrian wrote:

00:08:28.520 --> 00:08:29.580
"Let's not mince words.

00:08:29.620 --> 00:08:33.920
This will be remembered as one of the best
science fiction/fantasy movies of all time,

00:08:33.920 --> 00:08:38.020
and this backlash simply must stop, because
that's no way to treat a near-masterpiece."

00:08:38.340 --> 00:08:43.939
At the end of the day, The Last Jedi racked
up an enviable 91% rating on Rotten Tomatoes.

00:08:44.540 --> 00:08:45.699
"That's impossible."

00:08:46.560 --> 00:08:47.060
"Yes.

00:08:49.300 --> 00:08:50.200
And they've done it."

00:08:50.600 --> 00:08:54.480
Coming a full decade after the conclusion
of the prequel trilogy, The Force Awakens

00:08:54.480 --> 00:08:56.980
was greeted warmly by critics and fans alike.

00:08:57.160 --> 00:09:01.540
Its new characters were found to be compelling
and relatable, its villain was surprisingly

00:09:01.540 --> 00:09:05.730
complex, and it featured the return of the
beloved principals from the original trilogy,

00:09:05.730 --> 00:09:07.400
most of them in major roles.

00:09:07.500 --> 00:09:11.339
Sure, the plot beats might have been a touch
familiar, but that's easy to forgive when

00:09:11.340 --> 00:09:12.960
a movie is this well-crafted.

00:09:13.340 --> 00:09:16.960
Critics certainly thought the film was a major
improvement on the prequel trilogy.

00:09:17.460 --> 00:09:18.360
"Good job, kid.

00:09:19.660 --> 00:09:20.160
Thanks."

00:09:20.820 --> 00:09:21.320
"You're welcome."

00:09:24.420 --> 00:09:27.060
In his review for The Atlantic, Christopher
Orr wrote:

00:09:27.480 --> 00:09:31.319
"With The Force Awakens, [J.J.] Abrams has
begun one of the most important reclamation

00:09:31.319 --> 00:09:35.869
projects of our time: the complete erasure
from cultural memory of The Phantom Menace

00:09:35.869 --> 00:09:36.740
and its sequels."

00:09:36.960 --> 00:09:41.000
Generally speaking, critics thought The Force
Awakens was a thrilling return to form, and

00:09:41.000 --> 00:09:42.940
an excellent Star Wars film in its own right.

00:09:43.360 --> 00:09:46.780
Ultimately, the film scored an impressive
93% on Rotten Tomatoes.

00:09:47.560 --> 00:09:50.600
What can be said about A New Hope that hasn't
been said already?

00:09:50.640 --> 00:09:53.060
It's a movie that won over an entire generation.

00:09:53.240 --> 00:09:57.520
One thing worth noting: The sci-fi opus wasn't
unanimously praised upon its release.

00:09:57.580 --> 00:10:03.100
In her 1977 review published in the Sydney
Morning Herald, Helen Frizell even bodyshames

00:10:03.100 --> 00:10:04.180
R2D2, writing:

00:10:04.440 --> 00:10:10.080
"Oh, the astronomical audiences; oh, the mundane
story; oh, the over-cute little robots (one

00:10:10.089 --> 00:10:15.050
fat, one thin); oh, the vapid actors, the
galactic shootouts; oh, the brilliant technology

00:10:15.050 --> 00:10:17.240
and oh, the boredom of it all."

00:10:17.380 --> 00:10:18.200
"What's wrong?"

00:10:19.660 --> 00:10:21.740
"I felt a great disturbance in the force."

00:10:22.060 --> 00:10:26.399
Still, most reviews were extremely positive,
with Dale Pollock writing in the Santa Cruz

00:10:26.400 --> 00:10:26.900
Sentinel:

00:10:27.320 --> 00:10:30.580
"This is one film that exceeds its tremendous
pre-release ballyhoo.

00:10:31.040 --> 00:10:33.319
Few films have ever been this much fun to
watch.

00:10:33.320 --> 00:10:37.560
[...] It conquers our dreams, lights up our
hopes, and leads us down the path to the future."

00:10:37.960 --> 00:10:42.360
Not surprisingly, A New Hope scored an impressive
93% rating on Rotten Tomatoes.

00:10:43.040 --> 00:10:46.980
The Empire Strikes Back dramatically raised
the stakes for our heroes and ended with their

00:10:46.980 --> 00:10:50.140
crushing defeat — a bold and unusual move
for a sequel.

00:10:50.520 --> 00:10:52.760
Critics were impressed with the film, to say
the least.

00:10:53.080 --> 00:10:55.820
Charles Champlin of the Los Angeles Times
wrote:

00:10:56.060 --> 00:11:00.200
"The Empire Strikes Back suggests strongly
that the Lucas imagination has hardly begun

00:11:00.200 --> 00:11:01.020
to be tested.

00:11:01.200 --> 00:11:04.480
[...] It is expansive but more tightly time-framed
in terms of plot.

00:11:04.760 --> 00:11:08.860
I wish it were a handful of minutes shorter,
but this is my single caveat about another

00:11:08.860 --> 00:11:12.320
richly imaginative, engrossing and spectacular
motion picture."

00:11:12.840 --> 00:11:16.880
In a more recent review, Jeffrey Lyles of
Lyles' Movie Files succinctly wrote:

00:11:17.080 --> 00:11:21.700
"See it, and prepare to be captivated by the
most spellbinding sci-fi film of them all."

00:11:21.700 --> 00:11:26.260
No wonder The Empire Strikes Back currently
holds a 94% on Rotten Tomatoes.

00:11:26.400 --> 00:11:27.400
Can you believe it?

